1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Welcome back.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This session continues the picture we've
been building.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals can all shape

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,560
communication, connection and

5
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:18,200
the energy available across a day.

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,400
Keep your own child in mind as we explore
these together.

7
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,640
Let's begin with communication.

8
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:29,120
Before thinking about anyone else's way of
communicating,

9
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:30,640
we'll notice how many different ways

10
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:32,200
we already use ourselves.

11
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:38,120
Since you woke up today, how have you
communicated?

12
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:40,040
Think of three different ways.

13
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:42,960
It could be a spoken greeting, a message,
a wave,

14
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:44,880
a look, pointing to something,

15
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:47,440
or taking a moment before responding.

16
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:48,880
Have a quiet think,

17
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,720
then share one example if you'd like to.

18
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:52,960
Pause here for a moment.

19
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:04,720
We all move between different ways of
communicating.

20
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:06,840
Speech is one part of that picture.

21
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:12,630
Writing, gesture, a device, showing a
photo or moving away can carry a

22
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:14,120
message too.

23
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:17,050
Keep that variety in mind when we consider
what helps someone

24
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:19,740
understand and express themselves.

25
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:25,120
The two sides of this slide separate
understanding, information coming

26
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:26,640
in, from expressing,

27
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:30,000
messages going out. How much someone
speaks doesn't tell us how much

28
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,720
they understand.

29
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:35,850
A person may speak fluently and still need
support to understand spoken

30
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:36,880
information.

31
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:41,280
Someone else may understand more than they
can readily express.

32
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:45,440
A A C means augmentative and alternative
communication.

33
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:49,690
It can include signs, a communication
board or book, typing or a

34
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:51,520
communication device.

35
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:55,680
Some people use it alongside speech, and
some instead of speech.

36
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:59,140
Communication can change with the setting,
the demands and how someone

37
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:00,080
is feeling.

38
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:03,920
Repeated words or familiar scripts can
carry meaning too.

39
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:06,400
Stay curious about their purpose and the
context.

40
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:10,880
As you watch this video, notice what
supports communication.

41
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:13,200
Choose one idea that stands out:

42
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:15,880
perhaps a communication method, something
another person does,

43
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:17,920
or something about the environment.

44
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:19,680
Let's hear the speakers' own accounts.

45
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:57,760
What stood out to you about what supported
communication?

46
00:07:57,760 --> 00:07:59,920
Take a moment to think, or share one
observation

47
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:01,240
with someone nearby.

48
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:03,640
Stay close to what the speakers said.

49
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:05,120
Pause here if you'd like longer.

50
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:15,600
Keep that observation with you.

51
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:19,120
There is no single Autistic communication
profile.

52
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:21,680
We'll now consider the people and places
in your child's own

53
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:22,720
communication world.

54
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:29,320
Turn to the communication network on page
nine.

55
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:32,890
Your child is in the centre. Around them
are home, school, friends,

56
00:08:32,890 --> 00:08:34,840
community and supports.

57
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:37,120
Add one person or place now.

58
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:40,120
You can write, draw or use a few words.

59
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:42,560
There is no need to fill every box.

60
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:46,560
A network can be small, changing or still
developing.

61
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:50,360
A grandparent, teacher or coach may see
different parts of

62
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:51,640
communication.

63
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:54,040
Pause while you add one starting point.

64
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
You can return to the rest later,
including with your child and other

65
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,800
communication partners.

66
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:12,770
This page helps us notice who is connected
and what they might need to

67
00:09:12,770 --> 00:09:13,240
understand.

68
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:16,760
There is no ranking of who knows your
child best.

69
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:21,800
On the same page, let's think about what
useful knowledge could travel

70
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:23,280
between people and places.

71
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:25,680
Purple is a communication strength.

72
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:28,520
Blue is something that helps
communication.

73
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:31,020
Orange is something you would like others
to know.

74
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:35,020
Use coloured notes if you have them, or
the three workbook boxes.

75
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:36,920
Write one, two or all three.

76
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:40,980
You can use a made-up example, and you can
keep your notes private.

77
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:45,160
If you share at a table or on a board,
leave out identifying details.

78
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:47,796
Pause now for writing and optional
sharing.

79
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:57,720
Look across those ideas.

80
00:09:57,720 --> 00:10:01,240
What strengths might be missed if we
looked only for speech?

81
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:05,440
Which supports involve changing what a
communication partner does or

82
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:06,800
provides?

83
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:11,680
Keep a blue note, or something from the
What helps box, in mind.

84
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:13,800
That's our link to the next slide.

85
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:17,320
Useful information can help a child avoid
having to start again with

86
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:18,280
every new person.

87
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:24,160
Here are three options to consider
alongside what you've written.

88
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:29,210
Clear communication means familiar,
specific words and one idea or

89
00:10:29,210 --> 00:10:31,120
step at a time.

90
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,640
Processing time means allowing space to
understand,

91
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:37,920
organise a response and communicate it.

92
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:42,000
Visual supports mean showing information
as well as saying it.

93
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,040
The useful support depends on the person
and the moment.

94
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:48,800
Try leaving a pause without filling it
with more words.

95
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:51,920
Keep the person's communication methods
available,

96
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:54,440
including when the message is no.

97
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:57,320
These are ways to make communication more
accessible,

98
00:10:57,320 --> 00:10:59,400
and we need to check whether they help.

99
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:05,000
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to.

100
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,560
On this slide, a timer answers how much
time is left.

101
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:11,360
A sequence answers what happens next.

102
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:13,720
A daily schedule could answer what is
happening today,

103
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:17,680
although that example space is blank in
the supplied slide.

104
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:20,400
You might already use a shopping list,
calendar

105
00:11:20,400 --> 00:11:22,200
or reminder on your phone.

106
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:24,240
What visual information do you use at
home,

107
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:25,520
and what makes it helpful?

108
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:28,440
Share a brief example if you wish,

109
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:30,280
or note one privately.

110
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:31,380
Pause here.

111
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:42,800
The helpful format differs from person to
person.

112
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:46,660
It might be an object, photo, symbol or
written words, on paper or a

113
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:47,960
device.

114
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:51,920
Check that the information makes sense and
is available when needed.

115
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:55,520
A visual should offer information.
Communication tools still need to

116
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:57,160
stay available in their own right.

117
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:02,680
Connection involves understanding in both
directions.

118
00:12:02,680 --> 00:12:06,540
The double empathy idea, introduced by
Autistic researcher

119
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:09,820
Damian Milton, asks us to consider
misunderstandings

120
00:12:09,820 --> 00:12:12,360
between people with different experiences

121
00:12:12,360 --> 00:12:15,820
and communication styles as a shared gap.

122
00:12:15,820 --> 00:12:17,780
The image shows two people sending

123
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:19,980
and receiving in their own ways.

124
00:12:19,980 --> 00:12:22,660
Both have something to understand.

125
00:12:22,660 --> 00:12:24,060
You may already do this at home

126
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:25,980
as you learn your child's communication

127
00:12:25,980 --> 00:12:27,860
and they learn yours.

128
00:12:27,860 --> 00:12:30,780
We can ask what we, as communication
partners,

129
00:12:30,780 --> 00:12:33,060
might change to make the exchange easier.

130
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:36,820
Listening and adjustment belong on both
sides.

131
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:43,040
Let's hear one person's experience of
being social.

132
00:12:43,040 --> 00:12:46,340
As you listen, notice what connection
means in this account, without

133
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:47,600
expecting it to describe

134
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:50,297
every Autistic person's experience.

135
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:38,860
Take a quiet moment.

136
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:40,220
What did you notice?

137
00:13:40,220 --> 00:13:43,080
You can share one reflection or hold it
privately.

138
00:13:43,080 --> 00:13:45,100
Pause the video if you need longer.

139
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:56,320
Carry that reflection into the next
discussion.

140
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:59,320
Connection can take different forms, and
the conditions around it

141
00:13:59,320 --> 00:14:01,120
can make a difference.

142
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:06,270
Social connection can happen in family
life, school, the community and

143
00:14:06,270 --> 00:14:06,740
online.

144
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:11,500
It may involve conversation, shared
interests, doing something

145
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:14,770
alongside another person, quiet company or
contact that happens only

146
00:14:14,770 --> 00:14:15,820
occasionally.

147
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:19,190
Sharing lots of detail about an interest
can itself be a way of

148
00:14:19,190 --> 00:14:20,940
reaching towards someone.

149
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:25,520
Families and cultures also have different
ways of being together.

150
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:28,980
Think about the connections your child
already enjoys.

151
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:33,240
We can look for what feels meaningful and
workable for them, rather than

152
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:36,460
using one style of interaction as the
measure for everyone.

153
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:42,600
On page ten, think about when your child
seems most comfortable

154
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:43,840
connecting.

155
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:45,260
What do they enjoy:

156
00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:49,880
talking, gaming, sport, making something,
shared interests, or being

157
00:14:49,880 --> 00:14:51,560
together quietly?

158
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:54,890
When does it feel enjoyable, and when does
it become tiring or

159
00:14:54,890 --> 00:14:56,120
overwhelming?

160
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:59,150
Then consider what adults could change to
make connections safer,

161
00:14:59,150 --> 00:15:01,820
easier or more meaningful.

162
00:15:01,820 --> 00:15:03,540
Start with your child's preferences.

163
00:15:03,540 --> 00:15:06,880
You can reflect privately or talk with
your table.

164
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:09,080
Pause for the discussion.

165
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:20,560
As we return, keep one condition that
seems to help in mind.

166
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:23,880
It could be the person, place, activity or
amount of time.

167
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:27,430
Some children have few connections right
now, and this is not a

168
00:15:27,430 --> 00:15:28,760
comparison between families.

169
00:15:28,760 --> 00:15:32,410
Meaningful connection can look different
across people, settings and

170
00:15:32,410 --> 00:15:32,960
days.

171
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:36,840
Now let's look at daily capacity.

172
00:15:36,840 --> 00:15:39,580
Something can be enjoyable and still take
energy.

173
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:41,880
Communication, sensory processing,

174
00:15:41,880 --> 00:15:43,880
transitions and social connection

175
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:46,780
all draw on the resources available in
that moment.

176
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:51,760
Think of energy as a battery. Each day can
begin with a different

177
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:54,300
amount of charge, and activities draw on
it.

178
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:57,700
When it runs low, ordinary things can
become harder.

179
00:15:57,700 --> 00:16:01,960
Many people also use the spoons analogy: a
limited supply of energy,

180
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:04,960
with different activities using different
amounts.

181
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:09,800
These are optional ways to describe an
experience, rather than measures

182
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:10,840
of ability.

183
00:16:10,840 --> 00:16:15,820
The important question is what costs
energy and what helps restore it.

184
00:16:16,660 --> 00:16:20,250
Rest, familiar interests or a
better-fitting environment may help

185
00:16:20,250 --> 00:16:21,020
recharge.

186
00:16:21,020 --> 00:16:25,350
Support needs to match the charge that is
actually available, not the

187
00:16:25,350 --> 00:16:27,520
amount we hoped would be there.

188
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:33,240
Capacity can vary across a day and from
one day to another.

189
00:16:33,240 --> 00:16:39,490
Stress, sensory demands, sleep, social
situations and other events can

190
00:16:39,490 --> 00:16:41,760
change what feels manageable.

191
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:45,520
The same request may be easy on one
occasion and too much on another.

192
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:49,230
Think about what seems to use your child's
energy and what lightens the

193
00:16:49,230 --> 00:16:49,480
load.

194
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:52,960
We don't need to interpret a change in
capacity as unwillingness.

195
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:56,950
It gives us a reason to look again at the
demands and the support

196
00:16:56,950 --> 00:16:57,800
available.

197
00:16:59,960 --> 00:17:03,300
Masking can place another demand on
energy.

198
00:17:03,300 --> 00:17:06,320
We'll hear a first-person account now.

199
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:08,500
Let the speaker describe their own
experience,

200
00:17:08,500 --> 00:17:12,220
and leave a little room afterwards to
reflect.

201
00:18:44,680 --> 00:18:46,680
Take a moment before we move on.

202
00:18:49,800 --> 00:18:52,680
The image places two moments beside one
another:

203
00:18:52,680 --> 00:18:57,140
managing at school, and arriving home with
very little energy left.

204
00:18:57,140 --> 00:19:00,520
Appearing fine may not show the effort
underneath.

205
00:19:00,520 --> 00:19:04,200
That doesn't tell us exactly what is
happening for every child.

206
00:19:04,200 --> 00:19:08,660
It reminds us to consider what a day
costs, including effort we may not

207
00:19:08,660 --> 00:19:09,440
see.

208
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:13,370
Changes to the environment and
expectations can sometimes reduce that

209
00:19:13,370 --> 00:19:14,040
effort.

210
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:17,240
We'll return to the after-school
experience in our next session.

211
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:22,600
On page ten, consider where your child
seems to work hard to appear fine,

212
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:25,760
and where they seem most comfortable being
themselves.

213
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:29,560
What could adults change to help them feel
more supported?

214
00:19:29,560 --> 00:19:32,260
Start with private thinking.

215
00:19:32,260 --> 00:19:36,300
You do not need to prove masking is
occurring, or share a personal story.

216
00:19:36,300 --> 00:19:39,260
Keep your notes descriptive and your
questions open.

217
00:19:39,260 --> 00:19:43,300
If you wish, discuss one practical change
with your table.

218
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:45,416
Pause here.

219
00:19:53,440 --> 00:19:56,600
Keep that possible change in mind.

220
00:19:56,600 --> 00:20:00,320
Recognising something now doesn't mean you
should have known it earlier.

221
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:03,520
We can learn from new understanding
without blame.

222
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:07,520
Making room for a person to be themselves
connects directly to the idea

223
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:08,880
on our closing slide.

224
00:20:11,920 --> 00:20:15,360
Across these two sessions, we've been
building a picture of your child.

225
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:19,750
How the world arrives through their
senses. How they notice detail,

226
00:20:19,750 --> 00:20:24,200
focus deeply and bring particular
strengths. How they experience

227
00:20:24,200 --> 00:20:28,360
internal signals, communicate and reach
towards other people.

228
00:20:28,360 --> 00:20:32,660
Managing those demands every day can take
a great deal of energy,

229
00:20:32,660 --> 00:20:35,000
especially when the surroundings don't
fit.

230
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:40,930
Sensory load, transitions, communication
across different styles, and

231
00:20:40,930 --> 00:20:44,360
sometimes appearing fine can all add to
the cost.

232
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:48,670
A child who knows who they are, and feels
valued for it, has more room

233
00:20:48,670 --> 00:20:52,340
for learning, connection and
self-understanding.

234
00:20:52,340 --> 00:20:54,180
Belonging matters.

235
00:20:54,180 --> 00:20:58,490
So does genuine choice about how they
communicate, where they feel

236
00:20:58,490 --> 00:21:00,340
comfortable and when they need a break.

237
00:21:00,340 --> 00:21:04,630
Knowing other Autistic people can offer
connection and a sense of

238
00:21:04,630 --> 00:21:05,640
shared experience.

239
00:21:06,760 --> 00:21:09,840
Think back to the strengths and joys you
wrote down this morning.

240
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:13,700
Supporting a positive Autistic identity
can begin with listening,

241
00:21:13,700 --> 00:21:17,520
making room for those strengths, and
showing that this whole person is

242
00:21:17,520 --> 00:21:18,660
worth knowing.

243
00:21:18,660 --> 00:21:20,880
We'll pause here.

244
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:22,820
Take your lunch break when you're ready.

245
00:21:22,820 --> 00:21:25,620
When we return, we'll consider what this
understanding can look like at

246
00:21:25,620 --> 00:21:26,840
home and in the community.
